\f0\fs30 \cf2 \cb3 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
what parameter can I vary to make the pink_noise to sound more or less like a pure tone?\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
The parameter is 
\f2\b Q
\f1\b0 , the third inlet. It sets how narrow each resonator's passband is: bandwidth (Hz) = center frequency \'f7 Q.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f2\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Q \uc0\u8776  1\'965:
\f1\b0  wide bands, so it sounds like coloured, breathy noise.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Q \uc0\u8776  10\'9640:
\f1\b0  pitched noise, like wind across a bottle. Your default is 40.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Q \uc0\u8776  100\'96500+:
\f1\b0  very narrow bands, so each partial is close to a sine wave. What's left of the noise is a slow wobble in amplitude, and its rate is roughly the bandwidth. For example, 220 Hz at Q 220 has a bandwidth of about 1 Hz, so the tone wavers about once a second.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Two other settings decide whether high Q gives a pure tone or a pitched complex sound:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f2\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Partial amplitudes:
\f1\b0  high Q gives you eight near-sines, which is a harmonic tone, not a pure one. For a single pure tone, set the multislider to 
\f3\fs26 1 0 0 0 0 0 0 0
\f1\fs24  so only the fundamental sounds.\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Stretch:
\f1\b0  at your current 0.02, the partials sit within about a semitone of each other. They beat against each other, which sounds rough rather than pure. Use stretch = 1 for a clean harmonic tone.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Raising Q also makes the sound quieter, because a narrower band lets through less noise energy. To keep the loudness roughly even as you sweep Q, scale the master gain by about \uc0\u8730 Q. In the patch, that means a 
\f3\fs26 [sqrt]
\f1\fs24  
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  
\f3\fs26 [* 0.03]
\f1\fs24  chain feeding the 
\f3\fs26 *~
\f1\fs24  gain alongside Q. I can add that compensation to 
\f3\fs26 pink_tonal_bank.maxpat
\f1\fs24  if you want.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs30 \cf2 \cb3 what does stretch control, sonically?\cb1 \
\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 Stretch sets how the eight partials are spaced: partial n sits at f
\f5 \uc0\u8320 
\f1  \'b7 n
\f5 \uc0\u738 
\f1 , where f
\f5 \uc0\u8320 
\f1  is the tonal center. The fundamental (n = 1) stays at f
\f5 \uc0\u8320 
\f1  for every stretch value; only the partials above it move. Musically, stretch moves the sound between a single pitch, a chord, and a bell.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f2\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
s = 1, harmonic.
\f1\b0  The partials are at 1, 2, 3 \'85 8 \'d7 f
\f5 \uc0\u8320 
\f1 . They fuse into one clear pitch, like a bowed string or a voice.\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
s slightly above 1, e.g. 1.002\'961.02.
\f1\b0  The upper partials drift sharp, increasingly so the higher they go. This sounds like the stretch of a stiff piano string: brighter, with a slight shimmer, and still one pitch.\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
s well above 1, e.g. 1.1\'961.5.
\f1\b0  The partials spread far apart and stop lining up as harmonics. You hear a metallic, bell- or gong-like sound with an ambiguous pitch, more of a "chord" made of overtones.\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
s below 1, e.g. 0.5\'960.9.
\f1\b0  The partials squeeze together. At 0.5 they are at 1, 1.41, 1.73, 2, 2.24 \'85 \'d7 f
\f5 \uc0\u8320 
\f1 , which gives a dense, inharmonic cluster that sounds like a muddy chord.\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
s close to 0, e.g. your 0.02.
\f1\b0  All eight partials crowd within about a semitone of f
\f5 \uc0\u8320 
\f1 , the 8th at only 8^0.02 \uc0\u8776  1.04 \'d7 f
\f5 \uc0\u8320 
\f1 . You hear one blurry pitch that beats, chorus-like. Smaller s gives slower beating.\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
s = 0.
\f1\b0  All eight filters sit exactly on f
\f5 \uc0\u8320 
\f1 , which gives a single pitch. Combined with a high Q, this is the most direct way to get a near-pure tone. It is also much louder, because all eight bands add up.\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Negative s.
\f1\b0  The partials fall below f
\f5 \uc0\u8320 
\f1 ; at s = \uc0\u8722 1 they are at f
\f5 \uc0\u8320 
\f1 /2, f
\f5 \uc0\u8320 
\f1 /3 \'85, the "undertone" series. This gives a dark, low, subharmonic quality. Anything below 20 Hz is clipped.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Q and stretch work as a pair: Q sets how tonal each partial is (noisy versus sine-like), and stretch sets how the partials fit together (fused, beating, or bell-like).\
}